<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Wen of Han</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/203-bce">203</a>–157 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Liu Heng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Wen of Han, born Liu Heng, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> who was made King of Dai before being chosen emperor in 180 BCE after the fall of the Lü clan. His reign, to 157 BCE, was remembered as a model of frugal and lenient government: he lowered taxes, abolished mutilating punishments and avoided war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-han.jpg" alt="Emperor Wen of Han" width="320" height="528" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EmperorWenOfHan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Legalist and Daoist ideas merged in Huang-Lao, the dominant ideology of the early Han dynasty; Sima Qian held Shen Buhai and Han Fei to be rooted in it, and their practice of xing-ming re-emerged under <b>Emperor Wen of Han</b>, “cautious, unobtrusive and firm”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Dai passed to the Han Dynasty and later became a Han kingdom held by Liu Bang's son, the future <b>Emperor Wen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dai-206-bc">Kingdom of Dai (206 BC)</a></li><li><blockquote>His concubine Bo Ji later bore Liu Bang a son, the future <b>Emperor Wen of Han</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-wei-206-bc">Western Wei (206 BC)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=-174" aria-label="Chang'an on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7319 921 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emperor Wen of Han"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7769 1191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=-174">What was here in 174 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Heaven, it is said, covers no one in particular; and Earth is the common resting-place of all men.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the chief of the Xiongnu (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let us make a fresh start. Let us secure quiet to the aged; and to the young, opportunity to grow up, and, without risk of harm, to complete their allotted span.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the chief of the Xiongnu (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The rulers of old never broke the faith of their treaties. O great Captain, remember this. And when peace shall prevail once more, rest assured that its first breach will not proceed from the House of Han.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the chief of the Xiongnu (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
